<%# THE contest_entry INTENT — rendered from layouts/application, on EVERY page.
    That is deliberate and it is the fix for the blocker below.

    WHY NOT IN THE BOARD PARTIAL, where this started. The board renders only on
    contests/show and contests/world_cup. A wallet returns to
    /auth/phantom/callback, which renders studio-engine's view — this app ships no
    local override — so the handler map on that document was EMPTY while the
    registry itself was present. walletOps.resume() consumes the journal at take()
    BEFORE the signTransaction hop reaches requireHandler, so the throw arrived
    with nothing left to retry. The user approved the transaction in their wallet,
    came back, and the entry was silently gone.

    WHY NOT THE OTHER FIX, which review offered as the alternative: have the engine
    callback render a host-supplied registration partial. That is the cleaner
    separation and it is the right long-term shape — but it needs a studio-engine
    change, a release, and ANOTHER gem floor on a chain already deep with
    silent-failure notes. Days, while entries are lost. This costs the handlers'
    JS on every page and gets every FUTURE intent working on the callback for free.

    Registration is idempotent and guarded, so rendering it everywhere is safe: a
    page without solana_studio/wallet_ops.js registers nothing and throws nothing. %>
<script>
// THE contest_entry INTENT — registered here, at page load, BY NAME.
//
// WHY BY NAME AND NOT AS A CLOSURE, which is the whole reason walletOps has this
// shape: on the redirect transport the page is DESTROYED between preparing the
// transaction and getting it back signed. A closure cannot survive that. The name
// is the only thing that can be written into localStorage and looked up again by
// whatever document the wallet returns to — which may not even be the same tab.
//
// WHAT CROSSES THE REDIRECT is a server-side SLUG, never transaction bytes.
// prepare_entry mints a PreparedTransaction row and hands back ptx_slug; the
// journal carries that plus the ids, and complete() posts them to
// confirm_onchain_entry. The row is the durable half, and it already existed for
// blockhash freshness — this flow just needed it to be the thing that travels.
//
// SIGN-ONLY, AND THIS IS NOT A PREFERENCE. prepare_entry returns a PARTIALLY
// signed transaction whose admin slot is deliberately EMPTY. The user's wallet
// signs its own slot and the SERVER cosigns and broadcasts (Transaction
// #cosign_wire). A wallet that broadcast it directly would hand Solana a
// transaction missing a required signature — and worse, we would never receive
// the signed bytes the server needs, so the entry would fail with nothing to
// retry. See /tasks/intent-declares-sign-only: walletOps currently PREFERS
// signAndSendTransaction where a wallet has one, which is wrong for every
// co-signed flow. It does not bite Phantom (its send-side deeplink is
// deprecated, so signingHop already falls to signTransaction) and no other
// wallet reaches redirect signing yet — but this comment is here so the next
// reader does not "fix" the preference back.
(function () {
  var S = window.SolanaStudio;
  // GUARDED like every optional capability in this app: a page that did not load
  // solana_studio/wallet_ops.js simply has no registry, and the entry flow's
  // inline path below is unaffected. An absent capability must not default to
  // the permissive branch.
  if (!S || !S.walletOps || typeof S.walletOps.define !== 'function') return;

  S.walletOps.define('contest_entry', {
    // SIGN-ONLY, DECLARED TO THE GEM rather than only to the reader. walletOps
    // PREFERS signAndSendTransaction where a wallet has one (wallet_ops.js:208),
    // which is wrong for every co-signed flow: prepare_entry returns a partially
    // signed transaction whose admin slot is EMPTY, and the server cosigns and
    // broadcasts. A wallet that broadcast it would hand Solana a transaction
    // missing a required signature — and we would never receive the bytes the
    // server must cosign, so the entry would fail with nothing to retry. The
    // prose below said this; the flag is what MAKES it true (:137 copies it onto
    // the intent). Latent until now only because Phantom deprecated its
    // send-side deeplink, so signingHop already fell through to signTransaction.
    signOnly: true,
    // Runs BEFORE the wallet is reached on the redirect transport, so everything
    // it returns must be JSON-serialisable — it is literally serialised.
    prepare: function (ctx) {
      return window.tmPrepareContestEntry(ctx);
    },
    // Runs on the callback document, in a page that never saw prepare().
    // Everything it needs arrives through ctx (the caller's own arguments) and
    // state (what prepare returned) — nothing from a closure.
    complete: function (ctx, result, state) {
      return window.tmCompleteContestEntry(ctx, result, state);
    }
  });

  // THE SIGNING HOP'S OWN redirect_link — the third defect this epic found only
  // on a real phone, and the one a green suite was actively hiding.
  //
  // WHAT BREAKS. A cold mobile session takes TWO hops: connect, then sign.
  // walletOps builds the second one inside resume(), as
  //
  //     signingHop(provider, connected.journal, {
  //       redirectLink: opts.redirectLink || journal.redirectLink
  //     })
  //
  // and NEITHER side of that `||` exists in production. studio-engine's
  // solana_sessions/phantom_callback — the page a wallet actually returns to —
  // calls `walletOps.resume(params, { navigate: … })` and passes no
  // redirectLink; and redirect_provider's beginConnect journals
  // dappSecretKey / dappPublicKey / intent and no redirect link at all. So the
  // signTransaction deeplink goes out with the parameter omitted (walletTransport's
  // query builder drops undefined values silently), and Phantom's docs list
  // redirect_link as REQUIRED on signTransaction. The user approves the
  // transaction inside their wallet and the wallet has nowhere to send the
  // signed bytes back to. Measured 2026-09-09 against solana-studio 0.9.2 +
  // studio-engine 0.74.6: hop two's query string is
  // [dapp_encryption_public_key, nonce, payload] — no redirect_link.
  //
  // WHY THE GEM'S OWN SUITE IS GREEN OVER IT: solana-studio's round-trip test
  // supplies `redirectLink: 'https://a.test/cb'` to resume() itself. A test that
  // hands the code the very parameter production omits cannot see the omission.
  // e2e/stub_wallet_round_trip.spec.js is the fix for that class — it takes what
  // the page really passes and judges the URL a wallet really receives.
  //
  // WHERE THE VALUE COMES FROM, and this is the part that cannot drift: not a
  // configured path, not a re-derived route — the URL THIS DOCUMENT IS ON. This
  // wrapper only ever runs on the page the wallet was sent back to, so
  // origin + pathname IS the redirect_link that worked one hop ago. A host that
  // mounts the callback elsewhere is right by construction.
  //
  // WHY HERE AND NOT IN THE GEM, stated plainly because the gem is the better
  // home: the real fix is one line in solana-studio's beginConnect — journal the
  // redirectLink so resume's existing `|| journal.redirectLink` resolves — and
  // that is a gem change, a release, and another floor on a chain already deep
  // with silent-failure notes. This partial's own header records the same trade
  // being made for the same reason (registration everywhere, rather than waiting
  // on an engine release, "days, while entries are lost"). Retire this block the
  // moment the gem floor can state that fix.
  //
  // GUARDED AND IDEMPOTENT like everything else in this file: absent walletOps,
  // nothing happens; rendered twice, the wrapper is installed once.
  if (typeof S.walletOps.resume === 'function' && !S.walletOps.tmRedirectLinkDefaulted) {
    var innerResume = S.walletOps.resume;
    S.walletOps.resume = function (params, opts) {
      var withLink = {};
      for (var k in (opts || {})) {
        if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(opts, k)) withLink[k] = opts[k];
      }
      // A caller that DID supply one outranks us — this is a default, not an
      // override, so a host or a later gem that starts passing its own wins.
      //
      // GUARDED ON location TOO. Every browser has one, so this branch is
      // unreachable in production — but the redirect transport is also driven in
      // node by three test tiers, and a wrapper that THREW on a world without a
      // location would turn a missing default into a broken round trip. An
      // absent capability must not fail louder than the thing it was optional for.
      var loc = window.location;
      if (!withLink.redirectLink && loc && loc.origin) {
        withLink.redirectLink = loc.origin + (loc.pathname || '');
      }
      return innerResume.call(S.walletOps, params, withLink);
    };
    S.walletOps.tmRedirectLinkDefaulted = true;
  }
})();

// SEEDS PER LEVEL, ON EVERY DOCUMENT. The board renders this constant into its
// own config and hands it to StateFanout, which is right for the inline leg —
// but the relay's painter runs on a landing page that may have no board, and
// StateFanout falls back to a HARDCODED 100 when nobody passes it. The board's
// own note says why that matters: the value is passed through "so the handler
// can survive a model-constant bump", and a fallback silently reintroduces the
// literal the pass-through exists to avoid.
//
// DELIBERATELY ABOVE THE HANDLER BLOCK. test/lib/contest_entry_intent_js_test
// lifts everything from tmEntryFetch to the closing script tag as RAW SOURCE and
// runs it in node — an ERB output tag inside that range would reach node as
// literal ERB and fail to parse. Interpolation belongs on this side of the line.
window.tmSeedsPerLevel = <%= User::SEEDS_PER_LEVEL.to_i %>;

// THE TWO HALVES OF contest_entry, split at the seam a page death runs through.
//
// Everything before the wallet is reached lives in prepare(); everything after
// it answers lives in complete(). They share NOTHING but their arguments,
// because on the redirect transport they execute in DIFFERENT DOCUMENTS.
//
// BASE58 IS THE WIRE FORMAT, and the conversions here are the price of that.
// The server speaks base64 (serialized_tx in, signed_tx out); every wallet
// deeplink protocol speaks base58. The gem's codec owns both directions, so the
// encoding lives here rather than being re-derived at each end.

// THE CALLBACK DOCUMENT DOES NOT HAVE window.authedFetch YET, and complete()
// runs there. authedFetch ships only in the DEFERRED importmap module
// solana_utils.js; studio-engine's phantom_callback view dispatches from a bare
// inline script during body parse, and wallet_ops calls handler.complete
// SYNCHRONOUSLY. So a direct window.authedFetch(...) throws a TypeError on the
// one document that matters — after the user has already approved in Phantom,
// and after resume() consumed the journal at take(), so there is nothing left to
// retry. That is the original incident reproduced at the moment of highest cost.
// Raised in review of PR 632; two of studio-engine's own callers on that same
// page already defend with the `|| window.fetch` half of this.
//
// THE 401 NORMALISATION IS NOT OPTIONAL. authedFetch answers FALSY on a 401
// (having surfaced the login modal); plain fetch answers a 401 Response. Both
// call sites below branch on `if (!resp)`, so the fallback has to speak the same
// dialect or an expired session reads as a successful prepare.
window.tmEntryFetch = function (url, opts) {
  if (typeof window.authedFetch === 'function') return window.authedFetch(url, opts);
  return window.fetch(url, opts).then(function (resp) {
    return resp && resp.status === 401 ? null : resp;
  });
};

// Prepare: mint the server-side transaction and hand back ONLY what survives
// serialisation. No Transaction object, no closure, no DOM — this return value
// is written to localStorage verbatim.
window.tmPrepareContestEntry = async function (ctx) {
  console.log('[contest-entry] POST prepare_entry (fresh blockhash + priority fee), currency=' + ctx.currency);
  var resp = await window.tmEntryFetch('/contests/' + ctx.contestId + '/prepare_entry', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'X-CSRF-Token': ctx.csrfToken, 'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'Accept': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ currency: ctx.currency })
  });
  // authedFetch answers falsy on a 401 and has ALREADY surfaced the login modal.
  // The inline flow returns here; a handler must THROW instead, because walletOps
  // is awaiting a promise and a silent undefined would read as a prepared entry.
  if (!resp) throw new Error('Your session expired — sign in and try again.');

  var data = await resp.json();
  console.log('[contest-entry] prepare_entry response:', { success: data.success, error: data.error, entry_pda: data.entry_pda, ptx_slug: data.ptx_slug, serialized_tx_len: data.serialized_tx ? data.serialized_tx.length : null });
  if (!data.success) {
    // CARRY THE PAYLOAD, do not flatten it to a string. A failed prepare is
    // often not an error at all but a BLOCKER the user can clear — no funds, an
    // unmet age gate, a missing first name, a wallet still to set up — and each
    // one has its own panel behind the board's _handleBlockerResponse. That
    // method belongs to the board's Alpine component and cannot be reached from
    // here, so the payload rides the Error back to the caller's catch, which is
    // in the component and routes it. Throwing a bare string is what collapsed
    // every one of those blockers into raw text on a phone.
    var prepareError = new Error(data.error || 'Failed to prepare entry');
    prepareError.blockerData = data;
    throw prepareError;
  }

  // THE OUTSTANDING PREPARED ROW, recorded where it is MINTED. prepare_entry
  // creates a PreparedTransaction with a fresh blockhash; if the wallet never
  // signs it, the board retires that row before offering "Try Again" so the
  // retry gets fresh bytes and a fresh wallet request. The board used to hold
  // the slug in a local because it ran the prepare POST itself — now this
  // handler does, on BOTH transports, and walletOps does not hand `prepared`
  // back when the signing hop rejects. So the record lives here, on window,
  // where the board's catch can still find it. CLEARED at the top of complete():
  // once signing has happened the attempt belongs to on-chain recovery, and
  // retiring the row then would be wrong.
  window.tmOutstandingEntryPrepare = { ptxSlug: data.ptx_slug, contestId: ctx.contestId };

  // WHAT THE USER IS ABOUT TO BE ASKED, painted here because this is the only
  // place token_funded exists — the SERVER decides it and echoes it back for
  // exactly this copy, and naming a USDC transfer over an entry the user held
  // "Hold for Free Entry" for would contradict the button they just pressed.
  // walletOps has no hook between prepare and the signing hop, so no call site
  // can paint it: prepare is the seam. Transport-neutral on purpose — the
  // redirect user reads the same sentence as their wallet app takes over.
  try {
    if (window.Alpine && Alpine.store('solanaModal')) {
      // THE SERVER'S ANSWER OUTRANKS THE CALL SITE, and the empty string is not
      // a currency. prepare_entry echoes the currency it actually priced, so a
      // board with no picker (the world-cup survivor board) names the right
      // token without repeating the server's "usdc" default in a view. ctx stays
      // as the fallback for a caller that chose one, and the generic sentence
      // catches the case where NEITHER answers — because the old expression
      // rendered "Approve the  transfer in your wallet...", token missing and
      // double-spaced, on every non-token-funded survivor entry.
      var currencyName = String(data.currency || ctx.currency || '').toUpperCase();
      Alpine.store('solanaModal').show('Sign Transaction', data.token_funded
        ? 'Approve your free entry in your wallet...'
        : (currencyName
            ? 'Approve the ' + currencyName + ' transfer in your wallet...'
            : 'Approve the entry transfer in your wallet...'));
    }
  } catch (e) { /* copy is a courtesy; never let it fail an entry */ }

  var bytes = Uint8Array.from(atob(data.serialized_tx), function (c) { return c.charCodeAt(0); });
  return {
    transaction: window.SolanaStudio.walletTransport.base58.encode(bytes),
    ptx_slug: data.ptx_slug,
    entry_id: data.entry_id,
    entry_pda: data.entry_pda,
    token_funded: !!data.token_funded
  };
};

// Complete: hand the wallet's signature to the server, which cosigns and
// broadcasts. Runs in a document that never saw prepare().
window.tmCompleteContestEntry = async function (ctx, result, state) {
  // WHICH DOCUMENT AM I ON — read BEFORE the line below erases the evidence.
  //
  // THIS IS THE TRANSPORT DISCRIMINATOR, and it is derived from the flow rather
  // than declared by a call site. prepare() sets tmOutstandingEntryPrepare on
  // the document that STARTS the entry. On the inline transport that is this
  // document, so the marker is here and the caller awaiting this promise is
  // alive to paint the outcome. On the redirect transport prepare() ran on a
  // document the wallet app destroyed, so the marker cannot be here — this is
  // studio-engine's callback page, which has no board, no component, and one
  // instruction after us: navigate away.
  //
  // WHY NOT A FLAG ON ctx, which was the obvious alternative. A flag is only as
  // good as every call site remembering it, and this flow has already lost a
  // real entry to exactly that class of omission — a redirect_link the caller
  // did not pass. Worse, a test would then have to SUPPLY the flag to drive the
  // redirect leg, which is the defect e2e/stub_wallet_round_trip exists to
  // retire: a test that hands the code the very parameter production omits
  // cannot see the omission.
  var preparedOnThisDocument = !!window.tmOutstandingEntryPrepare;

  // THE SIGNING HOP IS OVER — the prepared row is no longer retirable. Anything
  // that fails from here belongs to on-chain recovery, not to a discard-and-
  // retry, so the ledger prepare() wrote is cleared FIRST, before any branch
  // below can throw past it.
  window.tmOutstandingEntryPrepare = null;

  // SIGN-ONLY IS A REQUIREMENT HERE, not a preference — see the intent's comment
  // above. A wallet that broadcast instead of signing returns a signature and no
  // transaction, and there is nothing to recover: the server never got the bytes
  // it must cosign. Refuse loudly rather than post an empty body.
  if (!result || !result.signedTransaction) {
    throw new Error('The wallet broadcast this entry instead of signing it. ' +
                    'This contest needs a co-signature from the server, so the entry could not complete.');
  }

  var signed = window.SolanaStudio.walletTransport.base58.decode(result.signedTransaction);
  // Chunked, because String.fromCharCode.apply blows the argument stack on a
  // full transaction — the same 0x8000 window the inline path uses.
  var sb = '';
  var chunk = 0x8000;
  for (var i = 0; i < signed.length; i += chunk) {
    sb += String.fromCharCode.apply(null, signed.subarray(i, i + chunk));
  }

  // THE LONGEST LEG, AND IT USED TO BE NARRATED (board, pre-collapse). confirm
  // BLOCKS on send_and_confirm; unpainted the card still reads "Approve the ...
  // transfer..." with nothing to approve. Guarded — complete() runs on the
  // callback document too.
  try {
    if (window.Alpine && Alpine.store('solanaModal')) {
      Alpine.store('solanaModal').show('Confirming Onchain', 'Cosigning and submitting to Solana...');
    }
  } catch (e) { /* copy is a courtesy; never let it fail an entry */ }
  var resp = await window.tmEntryFetch('/contests/' + ctx.contestId + '/confirm_onchain_entry', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'X-CSRF-Token': ctx.csrfToken, 'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'Accept': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ signed_tx: btoa(sb), entry_id: state.entry_id, entry_pda: state.entry_pda, ptx_slug: state.ptx_slug })
  });
  if (!resp) throw new Error('Your session expired before the entry was recorded.');

  var data = await resp.json();
  if (!data.success) {
    // CARRY THE PAYLOAD, exactly as prepare does. confirm_onchain_entry answers
    // with blocker codes of its own, and the inline call site used to route them
    // through _handleBlockerResponse before it moved behind walletOps. Flatten
    // this to a string and every one of those panels collapses into raw text —
    // the same regression the prepare half was fixed for in PR 632.
    //
    // AND `code` SEPARATELY, because one board reads it without reading the rest.
    // 'tx_rejected' — the server refusing to cosign a transaction that did not
    // match the prepared entry — has a reassuring modal of its own, and the
    // survivor board branches on exactly that string. This is the only place it
    // exists: on the redirect transport the caller's catch runs on a document
    // that never saw this response.
    var confirmError = new Error(data.error || 'Failed to confirm entry');
    confirmError.blockerData = data;
    confirmError.code = data.code || null;
    throw confirmError;
  }
  console.log('[contest-entry] server cosigned + confirmed on-chain:', data.tx_signature);

  // THE ENTRY IS RECORDED AND NOTHING HERE CAN PAINT IT. On the redirect
  // transport the next instruction in this document's life is studio-engine's
  // `window.location.href = result.value.redirect` — so the free-entry token
  // count, the seeds fanout and the celebration card are all skipped, and two of
  // those three leave the UI asserting a figure that is no longer true. Write
  // the payload down for the document that lands, which is the only one that can
  // still paint. Measured on QA iPhone Safari 2026-09-09: entry 176 recorded on
  // chain, screen showed nothing.
  //
  // NOT ON THE INLINE LEG, and the guard is the point rather than an
  // optimisation: there the caller awaiting this promise paints immediately, so
  // a stashed payload would still be sitting in storage when its own success
  // card auto-navigates to the lobby — and the landing page would throw the
  // party a SECOND time. The inline transport must behave exactly as it does
  // today, which means writing nothing.
  //
  // FAILURE HERE IS SILENT ON PURPOSE. The entry is already confirmed on chain;
  // a device that refuses localStorage loses the celebration and keeps the entry.
  if (!preparedOnThisDocument && window.tmCelebrationRelay) {
    window.tmCelebrationRelay.stash('contest_entry', data);
  }

  return data;
};

// === THE RETURN LEG, LIFTED OUT OF THE BOARD ==============================
//
// WHY IT MOVED HERE. The three acts below used to live only in the contest
// board's inline branch, which meant they could only ever run on a document that
// was still alive when the server answered. That is the whole defect: on the
// redirect transport the board's document is gone. Here, in the partial the
// LAYOUT renders on every page, the same three acts are reachable from both the
// inline caller and the relay's painter — one code path, so a fix or a change to
// the celebration lands on both transports by construction.
//
// This is the same relocation, for the same reason, that the handlers themselves
// went through one change earlier; the partial's header records that trip.

// THE SINGLE DOWNWARD WRITER for the free-entry token count. Every other write
// to tokensAvailable in this app is an acquisition — nothing else lowers it — so
// skipping this leaves the wallet reading "Hold for Free Entry" over a token
// that has already been spent, and the next hold promises a free entry the
// server will correctly charge for.
//
// The board still owns a mirrorTokenSpend() method and it now delegates here, so
// there is exactly one implementation rather than one per transport.
//
// Keyed off the SERVER's token_consumed, never a local guess: the controller owns
// the funding choice. A null (cold-cache "unknown") count coerces to 0, which is
// the safe direction — 0 renders the neutral "Hold to Confirm".
window.tmMirrorTokenSpend = function (payload) {
  if (!payload || !payload.token_consumed) return;
  try {
    var sess = window.Alpine && window.Alpine.store && window.Alpine.store('session');
    if (!sess) return;
    var next = Math.max(0, (sess.tokensAvailable | 0) - 1);
    sess.tokensAvailable = next;
    if (window.updateNavTokens) window.updateNavTokens(next);
  } catch (_) {}
};

// THE CELEBRATION ITSELF — the three acts, in the order the inline leg has
// always run them.
//
// opts.source        the StateFanout discriminator, so a log line says which
//                    transport painted.
// opts.seedsPerLevel the board's server-rendered constant when a board is
//                    present; window.tmSeedsPerLevel otherwise.
// opts.lobbyUrl      overrides the payload's own redirect.
//
// THE ONE LINE THAT IS NOT IN THE BOARD'S ORIGINAL is the visible() guard.
// solanaModal.success() begins `var c = _onCurrent(); if (!c) return;` — it is a
// NO-OP when no onchain-tx card is open. The inline leg always has one open
// (complete() showed "Confirming Onchain" a moment earlier), so there the guard
// never fires and the sequence below is byte-for-byte what shipped. On the relay
// leg the page is freshly loaded and nothing is open, so without it every line
// after would silently write to nothing — the exact shape of the bug being
// fixed, reproduced one layer down.
window.tmPaintEntryCelebration = function (payload, opts) {
  opts = opts || {};
  if (!payload) return;

  window.tmMirrorTokenSpend(payload);

  var modal = null;
  try {
    modal = window.Alpine && window.Alpine.store && window.Alpine.store('solanaModal');
  } catch (_) {}

  if (modal) {
    if (!modal.visible) modal.show('Confirming Onchain', 'Cosigning and submitting to Solana...');

    if (payload.seeds_earned) {
      modal.seedsEarned = payload.seeds_earned;
      modal.seedsTotal  = payload.seeds_total;
      modal.seedsLevel  = payload.seeds_level;
    }
  }

  // OPSEC-007: no client to server PATCH of the level. The navbar reads
  // seeds/level from the seedsNavbar localStorage key this fanout writes,
  // already populated from the server response.
  //
  // GUARDED, because StateFanout ships in a deferred importmap module and this
  // function is reachable from a bare inline script. A missing fanout must cost
  // the seeds animation and never the card.
  try {
    if (window.StateFanout && typeof window.StateFanout.apply === 'function') {
      window.StateFanout.apply('seeds', payload, {
        source: opts.source || 'phantom-direct',
        seedsPerLevel: opts.seedsPerLevel || window.tmSeedsPerLevel
      });
    }
  } catch (_) {}

  if (!modal) return;

  // THE THIRD ARG IS IGNORED (application.html.erb): the engine's success card
  // drives its own countdown, defaulting to FIVE seconds. It is kept because the
  // board has always passed it and removing it is a change to a different
  // question than this one.
  modal.success(payload.tx_signature, 'Entry Confirmed', 999);
  modal.title = 'Good Luck';
  modal.lobbyUrl = opts.lobbyUrl || payload.redirect || null;
};

// THE PAINTER, registered by the kind the stash above writes.
//
// GUARDED like every optional capability in this file: a document without the
// relay registers nothing and throws nothing.
//
// WHAT THE RELAY LEG PASSES AND WHY. lobbyUrl comes from the payload, the same
// as the inline leg — the engine's card couples the CTA href and the auto
// redirect to ONE key, so withholding it to avoid re-navigating to the page the
// user already stands on would leave the primary "Contest Lobby" button inert.
// A five-second reload of the destination is the smaller cost, and it lands the
// relay user exactly where the inline user lands.
if (window.tmCelebrationRelay) {
  window.tmCelebrationRelay.define('contest_entry', function (payload) {
    window.tmPaintEntryCelebration(payload, { source: 'phantom-redirect' });
  });
}
</script>
